					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-82005#post-1556035</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-82005/scp-529#post-1556035</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Tue, 04 Sep 2012 19:20:10 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>YourBuddyBill</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1431579</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>This thing is hilarious. But yeah, I wonder how she can even move like that; she moves as if all four feet are on solid ground, even when two of them don't seem to exist. My best guess is that Josie's back half exists in some sort of coexistant dimension where all movement occurs in the form of the subject's choice. In other words, if walking was your attempted method of locomotion, you would walk through the dimension, and whether you were walking flat or uphill or downhill or even completely vertical would be up to you. If swimming was your attempted method of locomotion, you would swim through the dimension instead. The thin black sheet is basically a &quot;splitter/connector&quot; that puts the front half of Josie in this dimension and the back half in the coexistant one. Note that although in different dimensions, it would still be &quot;connected&quot;, allowing Josie to walk as if completely stable (if the black sheet was just a mobile portal with a cat stuck in it, both halves would be unstable and fall over). If Josie entered the water (which is unlikely because most house cats hate baths), the coexistant dimension would read her attempted method of movement as &quot;swimming&quot; because that's what she's actually doing.</p> <p>Anyways, I'd be interested to witness any water disturbance that may occur if Josie tries to swim (willingly; it's pretty hard to force an angry cat to do anything it doesn't want to do).</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
